WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.900
Directed by James McTeigue and produced by
the Wachowskis, Ninja Assassin is the story

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:11.209
of Raizo, an orphan who's recruited into a
shadowy clan of ninjas who carry out political

00:00:11.209 --> 00:00:12.269
assassinations.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:17.210
Ninja Assassin didn't do much at the box office
when it debuted in 2009, but since then it's

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:18.660
become a cult favorite.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.660
Why?

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.060
Here's a look at the untold truth of Ninja
Assassin.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:23.070
It started as a joke

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:28.390
During the filming of Speed Racer, the Wachowskis
brought in stunt coordinator Chad Stahelski,

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:31.100
who had worked with them on the Matrix trilogy.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:36.039
While working on a fight scene that involved
Korean pop star Rain, Stahelski had an epiphany,

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:37.120
telling Spire magazine,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.489
"We always kind of had a running joke with
the Wachowski[s] about making a kickass ninja

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:42.489
movie.

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:45.980
We told them we found our ninja, and brought
Rain in to do a fight sequence […] and everyone

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.480
was like 'Holy s***!

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.480
This guy is good!'"

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.699
Within a few months, that running joke was
in production under the title Ninja Assassin.

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:54.989
Rain did his own stunts

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.489
Many action flicks rely on stuntmen to do
the heavy lifting for big set pieces.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:03.820
But Ninja Assassin went the other way, as
Rain did almost all his own stuntwork, with

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.250
very little wire work or special effects.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.250
How?

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.560
Well, Rain started by training eight hours
a day, five days a week, for eight straight

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.659
months in order to pull off both fight sequences
and stunts like flipping off of moving cars.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:18.439
Now that's dedication.

00:01:18.439 --> 00:01:19.439
Inventive hardware

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.770
Ninja Assassin features one of the coolest
weapons ever seen in a martial arts film:

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:27.780
that blade-and-chain thing Raizo uses to hack
through mountains of ninjas.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.109
Although you can now buy it on Amazon, it
actually didn't exist before Ninja Assassin

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:34.700
— the production team invented it just for
Rain.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.110
Sure, chain weapons aren't entirely a new
concept, but figuring out the physics of their

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:44.140
modified kyoketsu-shoge required a lot of
trial and error before they hit on exactly

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.140
the right formula.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:49.520
And having an all-new type of weapon meant
a creative challenge for the fight choreographers,

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.829
who worked with Rain to invent a totally unique
fighting style.

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:57.070
Chad Stahelski said, "As we went through the
training, Rain kept getting better, so we

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.659
had to keep re-choreographing.

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:02.659
What we had designed originally, he outgrew
by the time we were ready to shoot.

00:02:02.659 --> 00:02:06.469
The more Rain's abilities developed, the more
our choreography had to evolve."

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:08.479
53 hour script

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:12.709
No movie works without a good story, which
is why most scripts are in development for

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:13.709
months.

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:18.440
Then there's the script for Ninja Assassin,
which Babylon 5 creator J. Michael Straczynski

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.960
wrote in just 53 hours flat.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.570
That speed was required because, with just
six weeks left until filming was set to begin,

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:28.580
the Wachowskis decided to scrap the script
they already had.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.360
According to Straczynski, after the Wachowskis
called him, he "went home and put on a pot

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.830
of coffee, and…wrote essentially a whole
new script in 53 hours.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:41.290
When a friend calls you and says they're in
trouble, you do what you have to do."

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.370
Sho Kosugi is a ninja legend

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:48.720
Ninja Assassin's brutal antagonist Ozunu may
not be a familiar face to you, but hardcore

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.430
fans of ninja films know that he's played
by none other than the legendary Sho Kosugi,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:58.050
who helped kick-start the ninja craze in the
'80s with films like Enter the Ninja, Revenge

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:01.209
of the Ninja, and Ninja III: The Domination.

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:05.909
Director James McTeigue decided early that
he wanted to cast Kosugi as the villain in

00:03:05.909 --> 00:03:10.430
Ninja Assassin as a tribute to those old ninja
films, and Kosugi was happy to come out of

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.150
retirement to play the part.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:16.530
The decision certainly worked, as Ozunu is
one of the most terrifying movie villains

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:17.799
in recent years.

00:03:17.799 --> 00:03:19.900
The orphans were random German kids

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.410
Some of the most intense scenes in the movie
are the flashbacks involving young Raizo training

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:27.360
with the other orphans under Master Ozunu.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.629
So how did McTeigue populate that mystical
dojo?

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:32.709
He just…found some random kids.

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:33.860
Well, sort of.

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:38.319
The movie was filmed in Berlin, so McTeigue
went around to all the local martial arts

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:42.181
schools and found a handful of kids who seemed
like they knew what they were doing, then

00:03:42.181 --> 00:03:43.181
trained them.

00:03:43.181 --> 00:03:47.599
In his own Hollywood way, McTeigue grabbed
up a bunch of children the same way Ozunu

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:48.709
did in the film.

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:51.049
Guess life really does imitate art.

00:03:51.049 --> 00:03:52.939
The blood was styled like anime

00:03:52.939 --> 00:03:57.049
Ninja Assassin has a lot of blood, but there's
a certain art to making all that gore look

00:03:57.049 --> 00:03:58.049
good.

00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:02.610
In fact, McTeigue studied classic anime hits
Ninja Scroll and Samurai Champloo to perfect

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.519
the stylized look of the blood.

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:08.250
After all, as McTeigue puts it, "what's a
ninja movie without blood, right?"

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.489
Amen to that.

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:10.840
A real heartbreaker

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.319
In the opening scene of Ninja Assassin, an
old tattoo artist explains that he's seen

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:19.519
the ninja assassins once before, and the only
reason he's still alive is because his heart

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:23.180
is on the wrong side of his chest — when
the ninjas stabbed him in the heart, they

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.180
missed.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.710
It may seem like a goofy gimmick, but that
heart-on-the-wrong-side thing is totally real.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:32.139
It's called situs inversus, and it happens
when a person's internal organs are flipped

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:34.130
around to the opposite side of the body.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:38.150
It's pretty rare, so yeah, the chances of
two people who are targeted by the same ninja

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.400
clan having the same medical condition is
a stretch...but it's not impossible.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.100
Even some celebrities, like singer Enrique
Iglesias, have situs inversus.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.130
So say what you want about Iglesias, but the
guy's pretty much immune to ninjas.

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:52.180
Thanks for watching!

